From 8b0833e95d4164d4f50e562719517e5d46def45f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Roscoe <bryanroscoe@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:41:35 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] AI: animate a man-land only if it can and would attack or
 block this turn

LandAnimationPolicy penalised an animation that would leave the land tapped,
and nothing else. The Yama deck lab saw the Easy AI animate Lumbering Falls on
152 of Tricky Terrain's own turns and attack with it on 40 (Lair of the Hydra:
145 and 14). Traces show the animations come before combat, often several
times in one turn, followed by an attack without the land or none at all.

The policy now gives the same critical penalty as the tapped case when:
- there is no combat window this turn: on the AI's own turn, attackers have
  been declared, or the land hasn't been under its control since the turn
  began (CR 508.1 + CR 302.6, the engine's summoning_sick flag); on an
  opponent's turn, nothing is attacking the AI (CR 509.1a; the window is the
  declare-attackers step);
- the land is already a creature (animating it again buys nothing);
- on the AI's own turn, the combat AI wouldn't attack with it: the animation
  is previewed on a copy of the state (Creature with the animated body's P/T
  and keywords; an X-sized body uses the AI's other untapped lands less the
  cost's colored symbols) and combat_ai::choose_attackers is asked whether the
  land attacks on the current board.

Tests: allowed before the AI's own combat on an open board; penalised after
its attacks, for a land played this turn, on an opponent's turn with nothing
attacking the AI, for a land that is already a creature, and for a 2/2 facing
an untapped 6/6; allowed once a creature attacks the AI. The existing tests'
land is now a permanent controlled since an earlier turn.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
